---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import ClaimsReference from '../../components/ClaimsReference.jsx';
---
<ToolLayout title="Standard JWT Claims Reference" description="Quick reference for the standard registered JWT claims — iss, sub, aud, exp, and more.">
  <ClaimsReference client:load slot="tool" />

  <h2>Why "registered" claims matter</h2>
  <p>The JWT spec (RFC 7519) defines a small set of standard claim names with agreed-upon meanings. Using these instead of inventing your own equivalents (like a custom "expiryTime" field) means libraries and tooling across languages understand your tokens automatically.</p>

  <h2>How to use this reference</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Look up the standard claim matching what you need to encode (expiry, subject, issuer, etc).</li>
    <li>Use the standard three-letter name rather than inventing a custom equivalent.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Common mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Inventing custom names for standard concepts</strong> (like "expiresAt" instead of "exp"), losing compatibility with standard JWT libraries that specifically check for registered claim names.</li>
    <li><strong>Putting sensitive data in any claim</strong>, standard or custom — JWT payloads are readable by anyone who has the token, since they're only signed, not encrypted.</li>
    <li><strong>Forgetting <code>exp</code> is required for token expiry</strong> — without it, a token never expires by the spec's own mechanism.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Use cases</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Designing the payload structure for a new authentication system</li>
    <li>Understanding an unfamiliar claim you see in a decoded token from a third-party service</li>
    <li>Ensuring your tokens are compatible with standard JWT validation libraries</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>FAQ</h2>
  <p><strong>Can I add my own custom claims alongside these?</strong> Yes — custom claims are common and expected; just avoid reusing registered claim names for a different purpose than their standard meaning.</p>
</ToolLayout>
